## 2. `resolvePaymentResult` — Return Shape Roulette (L21–32, `agent_risk` 0.44, highest in the file)

Evidence: *"3 object-literal return shapes"*, *"lowest key overlap: 0%"*, *"example keys: `{ code, message, ok }` vs `{ permanent, url }`"*.

**What an agent gets wrong today.** `ok` appears on two of the three shapes and is *absent* from the redirect shape. With no return annotation, TypeScript infers the union and callers reach for the obvious guard:

```ts
if (!result.ok) return showError(result.message);  // fires on redirects too
```

`undefined` is falsy, so a successful redirect takes the error path — and `result.message` is `undefined`, so the user gets a blank error. Discriminating on an optional field that only some branches carry is exactly the failure the 0% key-overlap evidence is pointing at. An agent adding a fourth branch (`{ pending: true }`) compounds it, because there is still no declared contract to conform to.

**Refactor** (`name_return_shape`): a discriminated union with an explicit return type.

```ts
export type PaymentResult =
  | { status: "error";    code?: string; message?: string }
  | { status: "redirect"; url?: string;  permanent: boolean }
  | { status: "success";  value?: string; cached: boolean };

export function resolvePaymentResult(input: PaymentInput): PaymentResult {
  if (input.error)    return { status: "error",    code: input.code, message: input.message };
  if (input.redirect) return { status: "redirect", url: input.url,   permanent: false };
  return { status: "success", value: input.value, cached: false };
}
```

`status` is present on every branch, so `switch (result.status)` narrows correctly and a `default: assertNever(result)` makes the next added branch a compile error rather than a runtime surprise. The explicit annotation is what converts this from inference the agent has to reverse-engineer into a contract it has to satisfy.

